---
import DemoLayout from '../layouts/DemoLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config';
import { buildHowTo, composeGraph } from '../schemas';
const steps = [
  { name: 'Write the page first', text: 'Create useful semantic content with a clear title, summary, headings, and links.' },
  { name: 'Add explicit metadata', text: 'Pass the page title, description, canonical path, and social metadata to SEO.astro.' },
  { name: 'Compose related nodes', text: 'Use a schema builder for the page type, then combine related nodes with composeGraph.' },
  { name: 'Inspect the result', text: 'View source and compare the JSON-LD graph with the visible page before publishing.' },
];
const schema = composeGraph(buildHowTo({ name: 'Build an inspectable Astro SEO page', totalTime: 'PT20M', siteUrl: siteConfig.site, steps }));
---
<DemoLayout title="Build an inspectable Astro SEO page" description="A four-step HowTo guide showing names, instructions, position, and a twenty-minute total time." canonicalUrl="/how-to/" schema={schema} eyebrow="How-to schema">
  <section class="panel"><p>Use this short workflow whenever a new route needs metadata and structured data. The four steps are deliberately ordered so markup describes content that already exists.</p><p><strong>Estimated time: 20 minutes</strong></p></section>
  <ol class="steps">{steps.map(({ name, text }) => <li><div><h2>{name}</h2><p>{text}</p></div></li>)}</ol>
  <section class="panel"><h2>Why this sequence works</h2><p>Writing, annotating, composing, and inspecting in that order keeps the markup honest. It also gives a reviewer a simple way to compare the page, metadata, and graph before a route ships.</p></section>
</DemoLayout>
